.TH CHMAP_DECLARE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_declare \- declare a hash map handle and its companion type variables
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_declare(hm_name, key_t, val_t);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro creates nothing. The handle holds no map until
one of the init macros runs.

This is the declare half of a declare-then-create pair. chmap_init(3),
chmap_init_mp(3), chmap_init_ch(3) and chmap_init_full(3) are the matching
create half. chmap_construct(3) and its variants do both in one step. They
are the shorter way to write the common case. Two separate steps are what
a handle needs when the code declares it in one place and creates it in
another. Examples are a map whose allocator a condition chooses, and a map
that the code creates only after the enclosing function checks its
arguments.

The macro leaves the handle and the companion type variables
uninitialized. The handle therefore holds no usable value, not even NULL,
before an init macro assigns one. chmap_declare_scoped(3) sets the handle
to NULL instead, and it also destroys the map at scope exit.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name  Name for the map handle to declare
key_t    Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and, with
         val_t, which implementation strategy the init macro selects
val_t    Value type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to declarations and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Expands to a sequence of declarations. Put it where a declaration is
allowed. It takes a trailing semicolon of its own
The companion type variables follow the scope of the handle. A function
that gets the handle therefore needs chmap_redeclare(3) there
Nothing is destroyed automatically. Call chmap_destroy(3) to destroy the
map that an init macro creates into this handle
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Every other type-inferred macro reads the handle. Do not call one between this
declaration and an init macro. Such a call reads an uninitialized
variable.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
chmap_declare(scores, char *, int);
if (use_custom_allocator) {
    chmap_init_mp(scores, &my_procs);
} else {
    chmap_init(scores);
}
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_declare_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_init (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_init_ch (3),
.BR chmap_init_full (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
